equacies of the law today then  could any number of critical studies and  reports if the law is indeed the  religion of America this book is sure to  swell the ranks of non-believers  Newsweek said even if you long ago shed  the illusion that trials are about  something as quaint as truth horror  makes you driven sits in this match  between science and justice new york  times this book leaves one wondering is  this any way for society to settle its  dispute Washington Post was just as done  of course not  Timothy Joe of the wall Timothy Noah The  Wall Street Journal the reader leads the  civil action convinced that the  courtroom is never a logical place to go  if you want to solve society's problems  even when justice is on your side well  those are interesting conclusions but  and I think there's a germ of truth in  all of them but they weren't my  conclusions I have because I've had to  I've come up with some conclusions  they're not terribly profound but  they're completely the opposite of well  not completely but largely the opposite  of what these what the reviewers were  saying now I happen to think our civil  justice system works as well as any  human institution can it is after all  organic conceived and operated by human  beings and it's constantly evolving  it'll never be perfect just like the  written word will never be perfect  because it never can be perfect I'm  gonna get a little heavy here but Thomas  Jefferson said that the price of  democracy is eternal vigilance and I  think that applies equally to our system  of justice what I learned in this book  was that the most important thing about  any system of government is its ability  to expose itself to sunlight to let  people like me journalists in to see  what's going on and to the extent that  it does that  I think it is healthy and I think it's  the best system we can devise as human  beings the book served as an odd mirror  for people's preconceptions and it went  to really kind of ridiculous extremes in  a way what happened last spring was  ralph nader's organization called up and  they wanted a hundred copies of the book  because they were deeply engaged that  that at that point in trying to stem the  tide of tort reform and they thought  that this book had an important message  that the US Senators should read  I have utterly no confidence in the fact  that a hundred copies of the book given  one given to each senator that the  senator is ever going to read it but but  Nader thought that the message in quotes  was important and he wanted to hand it  out at the very same time that Nader  called I think it was not the same time  but two weeks later Rush Limbaugh I  heard had spent ten minutes on his radio  show talking about the book  I didn't hear actually hear Rush  Limbaugh in fact I've never heard Rush  Limbaugh but somebody sent me a  transcript of what he said and he said I  quote I got totally it's I got totally  captivated by the book it's one of the  best books about how the legal system  works that I've ever run across  regardless of which side you're on  now how Rush Limbaugh and Ralph Nader  can both adopt this the message of this  book is quite beyond me I should add  parenthetically that Limbaugh went on to  say it's a little ambiguous but he said  now I warn you some of you won't like it  because it's about a group of  Massachusetts families who sue  industrial giants for polluting the  water and making their kids sick  now I admit that there's a little  ambiguity in there but I think knowing  Rush Limbaugh where it probably comes  down  I am personally about as far removed on  the political spectrum from Rush  Limbaugh as you can be but let's face it  he's got a radio audience of eight  million people and if he wants to extol  the virtues of my book and perfectly  happy to have them do so one of the  things that makes me the happiest of all  though is that I am friends of a sort  I'm friendly with nearly everyone that I  wrote about this case was a very  bitterly fought case there was a lot of  bad blood between the lawyers and a lot  of personal animosity as it happened  though I genuinely liked just about  everyone not everyone but just about  everyone that I wrote about they  genuinely hated each other though and so  I was worried about how the book was  going to be received by them one thing  happened when I began this book tour for  the for the hardcover last fall I went  into Barnes &amp; Noble down there the legal  area of Boston to sign some books  because I'd been asked to about by my  publisher and I got into Barnes &amp; Noble  and went back to the information desk  and I saw this man standing there bill  Cheeseman he was the lead lawyer for WR  grace in this case and I wrote quite a  bit about him not all of it flattering  by any means and he had next to him 22  copies of this book stacked up buying  them he was going to give him out to  friends and family when he saw me he  said I hide the author in Bennett said  he was wondering how he's going to get  him back to the office and I could carry  him for  so I did after seeing Cheeseman I went  up to see Jerry Fisher I had an  appointment with him  Jerry basher the chairman of the  litigation department at Halen door and  he was the arch he was the Nemesis of  Quitman of the main character the lawyer  who represented the families throughout  the case  Jerry faster told me that I'd gotten a  few things wrong he actually excoriated  me for about 20 minutes first he said  his eyeglasses aren't thick as I'd said  therefore it was pretty extraordinary I  actually I think it actually thought he  was kidding but his demeanor was not one  of the guy who was kidding and then he  said that he never took leftovers from  the firm's weekly luncheon buffet and  plastic garbage bag his ID said and he  said to me I always use paper plates he  told me that his daughter Mimi thought  that I had completely failed to capture  his warm and fuzzy side and then after  taking me to task for that for about 20  minutes he said but the best thing is  we're still friend and he invited me out  to dinner my feeling throughout this was  that I can write a better story if I can  see the world through the eyes of the  characters that I write about in to the  extent that they do that I try  faithfully to represent their point of  view one other thing that Patra said to  me was he said a lot of other things  actually but one thing he said was that  he thought I was too hard on judge  Skinner he didn't say that I'd gotten  anything wrong about Skinner just that I  was too hard on him in some ways I  regret that I think most people who read  the book come out of it feeling that  Skinner was the villain I certainly I  would have liked to understand why  Skinner did what he did I think I know  but the judge wouldn't talk to me he  wouldn't let me see the world through  his eyes I especially would have liked  to see jan schlichtmann through the  judges eyes it's a wonderful narrative  device for a reader to see for a writer  have the reader see a character through  the eyes of another person and Rickman  could be maddening he was abrasive and  he was aggressive and he was arrogant  and I had no doubt that the judge was  infuriated with more time more occasions  than one and I would have loved to hear  the judge talk about that Judge never  did and maybe that's because he he  didn't want to but more probably it's  because he couldn't I think he felt an  ethical constraint having been the judge  in this case that he could not comment  on the lawyers in the end I'm gonna take  questions in a minute let me just finish  by saying that I I began this book with  two aims and the first thing really was  to entertain people and then to inform  people I can't pretend to stand before  you or any group of people especially  lawyers as an expert critic of the legal  system but I did look at this one case  in great depth I took about a fifth of  my life looking at it and in great depth  and I think that I hope that I've shed  some light on how the process works the  process of our legal system in this one  instance and then the and in that  process I hope that the I managed to  engage readers and entertain them  because that was I think my first  calling thank you all very much for  coming  any questions yeah you know John when I  hear you talk about the slow process and  the the difficulties you had and the  writing blocks I remember how you were  when you were an editor at The Advocate  and I was working for you where  everything seemed to just pour out of  you very quickly you were you always had  your stuff ready on time you always are  encouraging us to get stuff in early I'm  just wondering when you switch to the  long form what changed I think that was  a great illusion I never had things on  time I didn't have to write many stories  being the editor of The Advocate and  that's one reason why to be frank I  didn't much like being the editor The  Advocate  I wanted to write I think the longer  form is much more complicated Okrent  told me I've been writing these long  stories for New England Monthly and they  were getting to 12 or 15 thousand words  and I thought that was pretty long I  mean I'd accumulate a huge you know  amass a huge amount of research on these  things and I thought you know I'm not  that far from a book Okrent old mate  you're a long ways from a book the  threads that you have to weave and a  narrative are a much more complicated  fact is in a story of 15,000 words you  literally can hold all the parts in your  head you can understand the proportion  in the pace of it in a book length thing  you know maybe you can but I don't think  so I couldn't at least not in this one  yeah could you suggest about how many  hours a day during those eight years  you've spent writing the book and by  writing I include such things as lying  on the couch I would say probably it's  really hard to judge an awful lot you  know I mean it occupies your whole life  I was really completely self-absorbed  with so you know when I ate dinner I'd  be thinking about the book when my wife  came home I'd talked to her about the  book it really was a full-time  occupation in fact they didn't do  anything else I taught at Smith and that  was turned out to be I think a mistake  because  it that occupied a lot of time I I think  I would have been done maybe a couple  months earlier if I hadn't done that  yeah two questions first  Lichtman gave you an awful lot of time  did he ask for anything in return like  money and second do you have any plans  to publish your journal I can't answer  the second very quickly no it really  isn't that interesting it's really this  long lament it's I wrote in it when I  couldn't write the other stuff though  it's it's pretty pretty boring Rickman  asked for nothing and my condition going  into this was that he'd not be able to  read the manuscript there was nothing  there for him he gets a story told I'm  always amazed when people agree to to  let me be around and be a fly on the  wall you and he said at one point that  he had nothing - in fact he said it at  the very beginning he had nothing to  hide let me tell you a little bit about  how this came about how I got it  involved in this charlie nut for those  of you who read the book you'll  recognize some of these characters  Charlie Ness and Harvard Law Professor  had been seduced into the case by jan  schlichtmann in puerto rico Nessen got  very excited about it and Nessen talked  to a friend of his I think they were  playing poker and Charlie was was  talking about this case and what a great  great thing it was how it was gonna ring  the alarm in the corporate boardrooms of  America and Roger Hoffman a friend of  Charlie's who's also a friend of Tracy  Kidder said maybe Tracy would be  interested in writing about this I got  in Tracy's the one who got me involved  in the book and that's why he said he  would see me through it or else so  Charlie called Tracy Tracy said he  really wasn't interested his father's a  Wall Street lawyer and I don't think he  has any interest in writing about  lawyers and he suggested me because he  knew I was looking looking for for a  book so I went in and I met with Nessun  and then I met with Rickman sitting at  that conference table that I described  in the book eight lawyers around me I  was working for New England Monthly  I was either gonna do a story or a book  I had no idea whether it really be a  book in my condition was complete access  and I had nothing to lose you know a  footman said no I truly wasn't that  interested in writing about this case at  that point I had no burning desire to do  that  the only thing that mattered to me was  the access I would not I guarantee you  have written about this hedge Lichtman  not giving me that access and they asked  for several things in return one was  read the manuscript before publication I  said no can't do that  and I felt perfectly comfortable saying  no everything they wanted because I had  nothing to lose I had nothing invested  Strickland got no money out of the book  he did get some money out of Hollywood  Hollywood had to purchase his rights in  order I guess they felt they had to in  order to make the movie so he got some  money there and some of the other  characters may have to for all I know
 

